<Project>

  <!--
    Centralized build settings. Values defined here apply to every project in
    the solution; individual .csproj files only override when they need to
    diverge. Keeps the "best-practice baseline" in one place.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest</AnalysisLevel>
    <AnalysisMode>All</AnalysisMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Generate XML documentation for every project, test projects included.
    (We can't gate this on $(IsPackable) here because the SDK locks in its
    decision before Directory.Build.targets runs.)

    The test project used to opt out. That made IDE0005 structurally unable
    to fire there, because documentation generation is its documented
    prerequisite (roslyn#41640) - halheinrich/Math#43. It now inherits this
    value and suppresses CS1591 instead, so the baseline means one thing in
    both projects rather than two things depending on which you are in.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Every ProjectReference builds in the configuration its consumer was
    asked for. halheinrich/Math#47.

    In this member the property is a pure no-op, and it is adopted anyway.
    Nothing escapes this repo: the only ProjectReference here is
    BigRationalLibrary.Tests to BigRationalLibrary, both projects are in
    this solution, so the solution's configuration map already covers the
    edge and it already resolves correctly. Confirmed rather than reasoned
    - Debug and Release outputs are byte-identical with and without this
    property, 2eb25db84fa69872 and 8dc523aee5947338 either way, measured
    at `-t:Rebuild` through the solution. It fixed nothing here and the
    comment should not be read as claiming it did.

    What it buys is that the baseline is one thing in every member rather
    than a property three repos carry and one omits, where the omission
    would have to be re-justified by whoever next reads the four files
    side by side. It is also insurance with no premium: the day this repo
    gains a ProjectReference that leaves it, the edge is already covered.

    Where it matters, and why the default is the wrong one there: in
    Microsoft.Common.CurrentVersion.targets on SDK 10.0.400, line 1584
    defaults ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform to true whenever
    BuildingSolutionFile or BuildingInsideVisualStudio is set, and line
    1609 hands it to the AssignProjectConfiguration task, whose job is
    then to rewrite SetConfiguration on every reference absent from the
    solution map, unsetting the parent's configuration so the referenced
    project falls to its own default - Debug. A consumer that references
    this library from another repo has no entry for it in its own
    solution map, so its Release build links this library's Debug output,
    with no warning and no error. Line 1584's condition names
    BuildingInsideVisualStudio as well, so that was never confined to
    `dotnet build <solution>`; Release builds inside Visual Studio linked
    the Debug producer too.

    Flagged rather than quietly relied on. This overrides an MSBuild
    default instead of working with it, and the property is not documented
    public surface; it lives in the SDK's targets, which is where it was
    read. The default exists so a solution configuration is not forced
    onto a project that may not define it. Every member here defines
    exactly Debug and Release with no platform variation - and the
    property propagates Platform as well as Configuration, so that
    AnyCPU-only premise is load-bearing, not incidental. If it ever
    changes, this is the decision to revisit.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform>false</ShouldUnsetParentConfigurationAndPlatform>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Lock-file based version pinning. The lock file (packages.lock.json)
    captures exactly the resolved package versions and is committed to git,
    so any clone restores the same dependency graph. RestoreLockedMode fails
    the restore if the lock file is out of sync with the csproj.

    Unconditional, deliberately. This was previously gated on
    ContinuousIntegrationBuild, a property nothing in this repository sets.
    The SDK does not infer it from a CI environment: measured on 10.0.400 it
    evaluates empty even with CI and GITHUB_ACTIONS set, and only an explicit
    -p:ContinuousIntegrationBuild=true gives it a value. The one workflow here,
    publish.yml, restores without passing it. So the gate never fired in this
    repository at all, including on the path that builds and publishes the
    package — a package could be produced from a graph the committed lock did
    not describe. That path is now enforced too, which is the intended effect.

    A file whose promise depends on a switch set somewhere else cannot state
    its own promise. Removing the condition makes the promise this file's own,
    which is the reason to prefer it over giving the workflow the flag.

    The failure it prevents is not hypothetical. A committed lock file
    elsewhere in this project recorded a dependency at preview1 against a
    preview2 producer for weeks, through four arcs, and every local build
    passed: a local restore *updates* a lock rather than enforcing it, so the
    drift was invisible to every gate. Locked mode is what turns that into a
    failed restore.

    Accepted cost: a deliberate package change now needs a restore told to
    re-evaluate the lock — `dotnet restore -p:RestoreForceEvaluate=true`,
    which is what the CLI's force-evaluate switch sets — rather than a plain
    restore. One command, once per intended change, on an artefact whose whole
    purpose is that it does not change silently. (The switch is spelled here
    as a property because an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen.)
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>true</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>
    <RestoreLockedMode>true</RestoreLockedMode>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
